WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.480
It's weird to say there's good news. Anytime&nbsp;
someone has died of corona virus, that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:12.240
doesn't seem right to say that. But I don't&nbsp;
know how else to explain this other than to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:21.520
we received a positive piece of news regarding a&nbsp;
tragic corona virus death. A Corona virus vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:28.960
trial participant got Corona virus and died&nbsp;
from complications. The news we got is that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:36.240
patient had received the placebo, not the vaccine,&nbsp;
which from the standpoint of vaccine development,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.600
is a good piece of news, although it doesn't make&nbsp;
this death any less tragic. So I'll catch you up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:46.160
on on what happened. One of the corona virus&nbsp;
vaccines being developed. That seems to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.920
I guess we'll call it one of the top three&nbsp;
sort of candidates. Is the Oxford vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.120
being developed and tested by AstraZeneca, the&nbsp;
pharmaceutical company? This is the trial that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.160
I told you. I don't know if it was four, six,&nbsp;
seven weeks ago that was being put on hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.880
because there was a serious health incident for&nbsp;
one of the people who received the vaccine. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.480
happens in vaccine trials and they put the&nbsp;
entire vaccine trial on hold in phase three.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:20.480
Tragically, that person died of complications&nbsp;
from Corona virus, which they contracted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:26.560
and it was a Brazilian participant. And that's&nbsp;
a horrible thing. What we've now learned is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:33.040
that this participant did not have the corona&nbsp;
virus vaccine. They were in the placebo arm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:38.320
of the vaccine trial. One of the important things&nbsp;
that's done in these trials is that not everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.600
gets the vaccine. Some people get the placebo and&nbsp;
they don't know it in order to compare the effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.920
of the vaccine versus the placebo, remembering&nbsp;
that this is a phase three trial. So what we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:54.560
looking at is not safety. Phase one and two has&nbsp;
already dealt with safety as the primary thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:00.000
that is being tested. Phase three is looking at&nbsp;
efficacy. Now, of course, if the person who died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.640
had received the actual vaccine, you would&nbsp;
have two things to look at. Number one, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:08.880
does it say about the efficacy of the vaccine?&nbsp;
Of course, no vaccine is 100 percent effective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:15.840
But number two, was the death related&nbsp;
to an adverse reaction to the vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.960
that would put the development of the entire&nbsp;
vaccine in question? In this particular case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:27.920
it was not the vaccine that caused this adverse&nbsp;
event because the person received the placebo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:35.200
and indeed, it was a complication to corona virus.&nbsp;
And the reality is that in a Phase three trial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:41.600
with 30 or 40 thousand people, if you follow those&nbsp;
folks for months, by sheer odds, there are people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.080
in that group who would die or who would have a&nbsp;
serious health incident. In this particular case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.040
the person actually got coronavirus. They had&nbsp;
received the placebo and not the vaccine. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:56.240
this is why we do trials. There are times when&nbsp;
an event would happen regardless of the vaccine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:02.720
in this particular case. This is an individual&nbsp;
who actually got the the the the virus that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:09.520
vaccine is being tested against. It's very sad&nbsp;
from the individual standpoint. It is, in a sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:15.120
reassuring from the standpoint of vaccine&nbsp;
safety. Now, there is also this discussion of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:20.400
if the person had gotten the vaccine and&nbsp;
still got coronavirus, would that mean that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.200
it's a bad vaccine and it wouldn't? It wouldn't.&nbsp;
Again, when you're testing 30 to 40000 people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.800
vaccines are not 100 percent effective. They are&nbsp;
not expected to be. You don't need them to be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:36.880
Order to be effective at the herd or larger&nbsp;
population level. But it does again raise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:42.880
these issues of the efficacy of giving placebos&nbsp;
to people. And this is a well-established area&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:49.520
of medical ethics. The question of at what point&nbsp;
would you say the placebo is no longer ethical?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:56.560
Because the real vaccine or the real&nbsp;
treatment is clearly so effective. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:03.360
that is a well-established area of medical ethics&nbsp;
that is sort of beyond the scope of this program.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:11.120
I don't have an answer specifically in this case,&nbsp;
but in general, placebos start to be questioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:15.360
when there is a standard of care already&nbsp;
existing that is better in this case. We're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:21.200
testing vaccines against nothing. The vaccine is&nbsp;
not a treatment and thus it's a very different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:27.440
sort of situation. And and then again, just as&nbsp;
a reminder, phase three is not about safety.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:32.800
It's about efficacy. But yes, when you give&nbsp;
the vaccine to 30 or 40000 thousand people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:38.000
you may get data about safety that you didn't&nbsp;
necessarily get in a smaller phase two trial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:47.040
All of this goes to say what? That we are still&nbsp;
in process looking at various vaccine candidates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:53.920
We still don't know how long will these&nbsp;
vaccines, presuming they work, confer immunity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:57.920
How effective will they be? Is it a&nbsp;
40 percent effective vaccine? Is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:02.480
70 percent effective? And then, by the way,&nbsp;
at the tail end of it, we really can't ignore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.080
that. There's a large portion of the population&nbsp;
in the United States that doesn't plan to get a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.880
vaccine when it's available, that doesn't have an&nbsp;
interest in being vaccinated. And how that affects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.520
our ability to get to herd immunity is both a&nbsp;
mathematical question, a scientific question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.400
and really a sociocultural question as&nbsp;
well, which we will continue to discuss.&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:27.840
Let me know your thoughts on this latest&nbsp;
news and your sort of outlook on the vaccine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.600
I'm on Twitter at David Pakman. Make&nbsp;
sure you are following me there.

